        {
          "id": "spf:defining-ux-design-operations",
          "label": "Defining UX Design Operations"
        }
      ],
      "Who Created This Method?": "The Design Sprint was created by Jake Knapp at Google in 2010, then refined with John Zeratsky and Braden Kowitz at Google Ventures (GV). The three shared the full process in their 2016 book, Sprint. Official source: gv.com/sprint.",
      "How Does This Method Compare to to Other Agile Methods?": "Unlike Scrum and Kanban, which run continuously, a Design Sprint is a short, one-week burst aimed at answering a single question. It shares the fast, test-driven spirit of Lean UX and Continuous Discovery but packs it into a fixed five-day structure. Teams often use a sprint to kick off an idea, then hand it to a Scrum or Kanban team for ongoing delivery.",
      "During While Kinds of Work Environments Is It Best to Use This Method?": "A Design Sprint works best when a team faces one big question or opportunity and needs a tested answer fast, before committing months of work. It fits small, cross-functional teams that can clear a full week and get access to real users. It is a strong fit for startups and new product bets.",
      "Steps to Perform the Method": "The Design Sprint runs over five days:\n1. Monday: map the problem and choose one target to focus on.\n2. Tuesday: sketch competing solutions on your own.\n3. Wednesday: decide on the strongest ideas and turn them into a storyboard.\n4. Thursday: build a realistic prototype.\n5. Friday: test the prototype with about five real users and learn what works."
    },
