          "id": "spf:defining-ux-design-operations",
          "label": "Defining UX Design Operations"
        }
      ],
      "Who Created This Method?": "Scrum was created by Ken Schwaber and Jeff Sutherland, who first presented it together at the OOPSLA conference in 1995. They keep its official definition in the Scrum Guide, maintained independently of any company, with the latest version released in 2020. Official source: scrumguides.org.",
      "How Does This Method Compare to to Other Agile Methods?": "Scrum gives more structure than Kanban, with fixed-length Sprints and set events, while Kanban focuses on continuous flow with fewer rules. It is broader than a Design Sprint, which is a single one-week event, and it provides the delivery cadence that discovery methods like Lean UX and Continuous Discovery feed into. For very large organizations, SAFe wraps Scrum-style teams into a bigger coordinated structure.",
      "During While Kinds of Work Environments Is It Best to Use This Method?": "Scrum fits complex product work where requirements change and the team benefits from a steady rhythm. It works best with a stable, cross-functional team that can commit to the regular events and that values routine and clear roles.",
      "Steps to Perform the Method": "Scrum organizes work into short cycles called Sprints, usually one to four weeks:\n1. Keep a Product Backlog of prioritized work.\n2. Hold Sprint Planning to choose what to build this Sprint.\n3. Meet for a 15-minute Daily Scrum to sync and adjust.\n4. Do the work through the Sprint and produce a usable Increment.\n5. Hold a Sprint Review to show the work and gather feedback.\n6. Hold a Sprint Retrospective to improve how the team works, then start the next Sprint.\nThree roles support this: the Product Owner, the Scrum Master, and the Developers."
    },
