        },
        {
          "id": "spf:defining-ux-design-operations",
          "label": "Defining UX Design Operations"
        }
      ],
      "Who Created This Method?": "The Kanban Method for knowledge work was formalized by David J. Anderson around 2010, building on lean manufacturing ideas from Toyota's production system. Its official definition lives in the Kanban Guide from Kanban University. Official source: kanban.university.",
      "How Does This Method Compare to to Other Agile Methods?": "Kanban is lighter than Scrum. It has no fixed Sprints or required roles, so a team can adopt it on top of how they already work. Where Scrum plans in time-boxed batches, Kanban manages a continuous flow. It pairs well with discovery methods like Lean UX and Continuous Discovery, and unlike SAFe it is meant for a single team's flow rather than coordinating many teams.",
      "During While Kinds of Work Environments Is It Best to Use This Method?": "Kanban fits teams with a steady stream of incoming work, like support, operations, or maintenance, and teams that want to improve without overhauling their current process. It suits work where priorities shift often and flexibility matters more than fixed cycles.",
      "Steps to Perform the Method": "Kanban improves how work flows through six core practices:\n1. Visualize the work on a board so everyone can see it.\n2. Limit work in progress (WIP) so the team focuses and finishes.\n3. Manage flow to keep work moving smoothly.\n4. Make policies explicit so everyone knows the rules.\n5. Add feedback loops like regular reviews.\n6. Improve collaboratively over time.\nTeams pull new work only when they have capacity for it."
    },
