      ],
      "Who Created This Method?": "Lean UX was created by Jeff Gothelf with Josh Seiden, who described it in their 2013 book, Lean UX. It blends the Lean Startup's build-measure-learn loop (from Eric Ries), Agile, and Design Thinking.",
      "How Does This Method Compare to to Other Agile Methods?": "Lean UX guides how a team designs and validates ideas, while Scrum and Kanban manage how the work gets delivered, so they are usually used together. It is close in spirit to Continuous Discovery, since both test assumptions with users, and it provides the fast, experiment-first thinking that a Design Sprint compresses into five days.",
      "During While Kinds of Work Environments Is It Best to Use This Method?": "Lean UX fits early or uncertain products where the team needs to validate ideas quickly instead of building a lot up front. It works best with collaborative, cross-functional teams that care more about outcomes than polished documents.",
      "Steps to Perform the Method": "Lean UX works in a learning loop:\n1. Declare your assumptions about the users and the problem.\n2. Turn those assumptions into clear hypotheses you can test.\n3. Design the smallest thing that can test a hypothesis, a minimum viable product (MVP).\n4. Run an experiment and put it in front of users.\n5. Measure the results and learn whether to keep, change, or drop the idea, then repeat."
    },
