      ],
      "Who Created This Method?": "The Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe) was created by Dean Leffingwell and first released in 2011 through Scaled Agile, Inc. Its official source of truth is scaledagileframework.com.",
      "How Does This Method Compare to to Other Agile Methods?": "SAFe is the heaviest of these methods because it coordinates many teams at once, while Scrum and Kanban each run a single team. It uses Scrum-style and Kanban-style teams inside a larger structure. Smaller, faster methods like Design Sprints, Lean UX, and Continuous Discovery can still run inside a SAFe organization to shape what each team builds.",
      "During While Kinds of Work Environments Is It Best to Use This Method?": "SAFe fits large organizations that need many agile teams working toward shared goals. It is best when the work is too big for one team and when coordination, alignment, and planning at scale really matter.",
      "Steps to Perform the Method": "SAFe scales agile across many teams. At a high level:\n1. Organize teams around value into an Agile Release Train (ART), a team of teams of about 50 to 125 people.\n2. Align the whole ART in PI Planning, a two-day event that sets shared goals for the next Planning Interval (about 8 to 12 weeks).\n3. Deliver in short iterations within each team while coordinating dependencies across the train.\n4. Integrate and demo the work regularly.\n5. Hold an Inspect and Adapt session to review results and improve.\nSAFe is guided by four core values: alignment, transparency, respect for people, and relentless improvement."
    }
